{"maxId":257,"entries":{"chapter-65-study-major-depression|Chapter 65 Study: Major Depression":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|~5% of older adults meet criteria for major depression — lower than in other age groups; new-onset depression peaks in middle age; among older adults, greater age brings a similar likelihood of becoming depressed but a higher likelihood of remaining depressed (p1007).":["u3"],"copy|In primary care, ~5% of older adults have clinically significant depression; about half are recognized/diagnosed; of those, only 1 in 5 get guideline-concordant care — so only ~10% of depressed older adults get \"adequate\" care (p1012).":["u4"],"copy|Depression is not a normal part of aging; normalizing it in older patients is misguided and unproductive (p1007).":["u5"],"copy|PHQ-9 scoring: 0–4 = no/insignificant symptoms, 5–9 = mild/subsyndromal depression, ≥10 = typically correlates with major depression and warrants further evaluation and clinical intervention (p1009, p1012).":["u6"],"copy|Figure 65-2 combines symptom count, depressed mood or loss of interest, duration and functional impairment to guide diagnostic assessment. Its chronic-depression row specifies mood symptoms for two years. For standardized PHQ-9 severity, 10–14 is moderate, 15–19 moderately severe, and 20–27 severe (p1011, Kroenke et al.). The score supports evaluation and does not independently diagnose major depression.":["u7"],"copy|Item 9 asks about death wishes or more intensive suicidal ideation — clearly an important part of the assessment (p1012).":["u8"],"copy|Two core distinguishing features of geriatric depression: anhedonia and hopelessness — they may be important clues when deficits in basic needs (Maslow's hierarchy, Fig 65-1) are more apparent than depressed mood (p1008).":["u9"],"copy|Social isolation (objective — network size, contact frequency) vs loneliness (subjective — perceived inadequacy of connection) are distinct but closely related constructs; loneliness is fundamentally related to depression, whereas some isolation (living alone, selectivity) may reflect capability and preference and need not signify a problem (p1009).":["u10"],"copy|Suicide/safety checklist (Fig 65-3) is triggered by PHQ Q9 ≥1, MADRS Q10 ≥3, or QIDS-SR Q12 ≥1 (p1013).":["u11"],"copy|Structured assessment: suicidal thoughts, intent, plan, behavior; risk factors (hopelessness, impulsivity, isolation, prior attempts, recent psychiatric discharge, access to means) vs protective factors (reasons for living, supports); document firearm status and an action plan (p1013).":["u12"],"copy|Assess whether a plan is formulated/rehearsed and whether means (especially firearms) are accessible — removing access lowers immediate risk (p1018).":["u13"],"copy|Even in \"gold standard\" collaborative care: <50% of patients achieve a depression response (≥50% symptom reduction) at 6 months, <1/3 achieve remission — two-thirds remain effectively depressed; between-clinic response rates ranged from <10% to >80% (p1012).":["u14"],"copy|Largest geriatric depression treatment trial: 51% of patients preferred counseling/psychotherapy vs 38% preferring antidepressants (p1014).":["u15"],"copy|Structured psychotherapy trials run ~3 months; if no improvement by 1–2 months, consider switching modality (p1014).":["u16"],"copy|In studies summarized by Hazzard, ECT response exceeded 80% and remission exceeded 60%, with favorable responses in older adults (p1014). The APA describes an acute course commonly given 2–3 times weekly, often 6–12 sessions. Confusion and difficulty learning new material usually improve, but some autobiographical memory gaps may persist.Current practice (not the book): ECT is typically given two or three times per week; an acute course often consists of 6–12 treatments — American Psychiatric Association (patient page).":["u17"],"copy|Antidepressants do not show benefit for minor/subsyndromal depression despite it having similar health/QOL impact to major depression — don't reflexively medicate mild cases (p1012, p1015).":["u18"],"copy|Antidepressants do not generally work in dementia-associated depression; favor behavioral/caregiver-support interventions instead (p1015).":["u19"],"copy|Slow up-titration gave much better adherence than a higher starting dose, even though both reached the same eventual dose; separately, dose-response research does not support a minimum effective dosage or narrow therapeutic window (p1015).":["u20"],"copy|Patients often don't volunteer side effects (especially sexual dysfunction) unless asked directly (p1015).":["u21"],"copy|In some nursing home populations, almost half of residents are prescribed an antidepressant — far exceeding actual depression prevalence estimates (p1015).":["u22"],"copy|DSM-5 defines major depression as an \"episode\" — expected to improve over time even without treatment, which complicates decisions about indefinite maintenance therapy (p1015).":["u23"],"copy|Reduce antidepressants in steps, with smaller reductions as the dose falls. Hazzard describes 2–4 weeks for many SSRIs, SNRIs and mirtazapine and longer courses for TCAs (p1016). NICE recommends adjusting the pace to the drug, duration, prior withdrawal and symptoms. Some patients need weeks or months.":["u24"],"copy|Fluoxetine has a long half-life. NICE allows a period of alternate-day dosing for some people taking 20 mg/day and recommends gradual withdrawal at 40–60 mg/day. A clinician-directed switch from another drug requires attention to interactions and washout (p1016, NICE NG222 recommendation 1.4.18).Current practice (not the book): fluoxetine can sometimes be stopped by alternate-day dosing at 20 mg/day; at 40–60 mg/day use a gradual withdrawal schedule — NICE NG222 rec 1.4.18, 2022.":["u25"],"copy|Discontinuation symptoms (sweating, chills, dizziness, GI upset, vivid dreaming, vertigo, numbness, shock-like sensations) can be confused with relapse/lack of efficacy — misdiagnosis may lead to unnecessary tests, useless treatments, and increased costs (p1015–1016).":["u26"],"copy|Table 65-1 (p1017) catalogs challenges clinicians face treating depressed older patients — emotional toll (feeling drained, like a failure), practical friction (patients' \"pulling teeth\" pace, bottomless needs, family members wanting to explain at length what they've observed), and slow response (weeks to months) — the chapter frames \"caring for the caregiver\" (including the clinician) as part of good care (p1016).":["u27"],"copy|A suicidal patient introduces medicolegal concerns — protecting the patient's safety and ensuring the provider documents fully (Table 65-1, p1017).":["u28"],"copy|Simple human presence (sitting down, eye contact, asking \"what else is going on?\") is probably the most important way to ensure older patients can express themselves (p1017).":["u29"],"copy|Medically complex, cognitively impaired, or bipolar/psychotic-feature depression is best suited for geriatric psychiatry subspecialty referral; most general psychiatrists should feel comfortable evaluating and treating older adults (p1018–1019).":["u30"],"copy|ECT should be considered early for severe or psychotic depression, or after limited medication response (p1014).":["u31"],"copy|Subsyndromal depression has real health/QOL impact but antidepressants show no proven benefit for it — don't assume \"some symptoms = start a drug\" (p1012, p1015).":["u32"],"copy|No trial has found medications more effective than ECT, and older patients seem even more responsive to it than younger adults — contrary to its negative-press reputation (p1014).":["u33"],"copy|Fluoxetine is the one antidepressant that breaks the \"always taper slowly\" rule (p1016).":["u34"],"copy|The PHQ-9 diagnostic table's dysthymia row requires Q2 (depressed mood) specifically, sustained 2 years — not just any 3–4 symptoms (p1011).":["u35"],"recall|Recall":["u36"],"copy|CHAPTER 65s / HAZZARD":["u37"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 65 — Major Depression; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u38"],"copy|Epidemiology":["u39"],"copy|Screening & diagnosis (PHQ-9)":["u40"],"copy|Suicide risk assessment":["u41"],"copy|Real-world treatment efficacy":["u42"],"copy|Treatment options":["u43"],"copy|Stopping antidepressants":["u44"],"copy|Provider & caregiver factors":["u45"],"copy|Special populations & referral":["u46"],"copy|Exam traps / book emphases":["u47"],"copy|1.":["u48"],"copy|What percentage of older adults meet criteria for major depression, and how does that compare with other age groups?":["u49"],"copy|About 5% — lower than in other age groups; among older adults, greater age brings a similar likelihood of becoming depressed but a higher likelihood of remaining depressed (p1007).":["u50"],"copy|2.":["u51"],"copy|What PHQ-9 total score range is generally considered to indicate no or clinically insignificant depressive symptoms, and what range indicates mild/subsyndromal depression?":["u52"],"copy|0–4 = no/insignificant symptoms; 5–9 = mild, generally subsyndromal, depression (p1009, p1012).":["u53"],"copy|4.":["u54"],"copy|At what PHQ-9 score range is major depression classified as moderate, and at what range is it classified as severe?":["u55"],"copy|The validated PHQ-9 grades are 10–14 moderate, 15–19 moderately severe and 20–27 severe.":["u56"],"copy|Figure 65-2 prints “moderate” for 15–19 and “20” for severe, which differs from the validated severity bands (p1011, Kroenke et al.).":["u57"],"copy|5.":["u58"],"copy|What three screening-tool thresholds trigger the suicide/safety risk factor checklist?":["u59"],"copy|PHQ Question 9 ≥1, MADRS Question 10 ≥3, or QIDS-SR Question 12 ≥1 (p1013).":["u60"],"copy|6.":["u61"],"copy|In the largest treatment trial of geriatric depression to date, what proportion of patients preferred psychotherapy/counseling versus antidepressant medications?":["u62"],"copy|51% preferred counseling or psychotherapy; 38% preferred antidepressant medications (p1014).":["u63"],"copy|7.":["u64"],"copy|Even in \"gold standard\" collaborative care, what fraction of patients achieve a depression response at 6 months, and what fraction remain effectively depressed?":["u65"],"copy|Fewer than half achieve a depression response (≥50% symptom reduction); fewer than a third achieve remission — roughly two-thirds remain effectively depressed (p1012).":["u66"],"copy|8.":["u67"],"copy|What are the response and remission rates for ECT in treating depression, and how does its effectiveness compare with medications?":["u68"],"copy|Response rate >80%, remission rate >60% — no trial has ever found medications more effective than ECT, and older adults respond even better than younger adults (p1014).":["u69"],"copy|9.":["u70"],"copy|What is the typical initial ECT course (frequency and duration), and what happens to short-term memory loss?":["u71"],"copy|The APA describes two or three ECT treatments per week, often 6–12 treatments, individualized to response.":["u72"],"copy|Post-treatment confusion and difficulty retaining new information usually improve.":["u72"],"copy|Some memory gaps for events around the treatment period can persist.":["u72"],"copy|The chapter gives a different historical frequency (p1014).":["u72"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): ECT is typically given two or three times per week; an acute course often consists of 6–12 treatments — American Psychiatric Association (patient page).":["u73"],"copy|10.":["u74"],"copy|Do antidepressant medications show proven benefit for minor/subsyndromal depression, and how well do they generally work for depression in dementia?":["u75"],"copy|No clear antidepressant benefit for subsyndromal depression despite similar health impact to major depression, and antidepressants do not generally work for depression in the setting of dementia (p1012, p1015).":["u76"],"copy|12.":["u77"],"copy|Roughly what proportion of nursing home residents are prescribed an antidepressant, and how does that compare with estimated depression prevalence in that population?":["u78"],"copy|Almost half of residents in some nursing home populations are prescribed an antidepressant — far higher than any estimate of actual depression prevalence (p1015).":["u79"],"copy|13.":["u80"],"copy|How should antidepressant tapering be individualized, and how does fluoxetine's long half-life affect dose reduction?":["u81"],"copy|Reduce in steps guided by symptoms, half-life and duration of treatment.":["u82"],"copy|The book gives 2–4 weeks as an example for several agents and longer tapers for TCAs (p1016).":["u83"],"copy|NICE notes that 20 mg/day of fluoxetine can sometimes be reduced with alternate-day dosing, while 40–60 mg/day requires gradual reduction.":["u84"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): fluoxetine can sometimes be stopped by alternate-day dosing at 20 mg/day; at 40–60 mg/day use a gradual withdrawal schedule — NICE NG222 rec 1.4.18, 2022.":["u85"],"copy|14.":["u86"],"copy|When assessing a patient with suicidal ideation, what two specific factors most raise immediate risk, and what action reduces that risk?":["u87"],"copy|A specified, rehearsed plan and accessible means (especially firearms) most raise immediate risk; removing access to the means (eg, the firearm) decreases immediate risk (p1018).":["u88"],"copy|15.":["u89"],"copy|Which patients with depression are best suited for geriatric psychiatry subspecialty referral rather than general psychiatry?":["u90"],"copy|Medically complex or cognitively impaired patients, and those whose major depressive episode is related to bipolar disorder or has psychotic features (p1019).":["u91"],"copy|Answer":["u92","u93","u94","u95","u96","u97","u98","u99","u100","u101","u102","u103","u104"],"copy|In the studies summarized by Hazzard, ECT response exceeded 80% and remission exceeded 60%, with favorable responses in older adults. The average initial course was 3–6 treatments per week for 2–4 weeks. Short-term memory loss almost always resolved within a few weeks after the course (p1014).Current practice (not the book): ECT is commonly given 2–3 times weekly, often for 6–12 treatments. Some patients experience longer-lasting gaps in autobiographical memory — American Psychiatric Association, What Is Electroconvulsive Therapy?.":["u17"],"copy|Hazzard gives 2–4 weeks as a rough taper for most SSRIs, SNRIs and mirtazapine. It gives several months for tricyclics (p1016).":["u251"],"copy|Hazzard states that fluoxetine does not require tapering because of its long half-life. If withdrawal symptoms occur despite a long taper of an antidepressant, the chapter suggests considering a single 20 mg dose of fluoxetine (p1016).Current practice (not the book): reduce doses in stages, tailoring the pace to the medicine and symptoms. For fluoxetine 20 mg/day, alternate-day dosing can be a step down. At 40–60 mg/day, withdraw gradually — NICE NG222, recommendations 1.4.16–1.4.18, 2022.":["u252"],"copy|Discontinuation symptoms (sweating, chills, dizziness, flu-like symptoms, GI upset, insomnia, vivid and excessive dreaming, depressed mood, vertigo, numbness, shock-like sensations) can be confused with relapse/lack of efficacy — misdiagnosis may lead to unnecessary tests, useless treatments, and increased costs (p1015–1016).":["u26"],"copy|Hazzard gives an average initial course of 3–6 treatments per week for 2–4 weeks (p1014).":["u193"],"copy|Assess medical and anesthetic suitability.":["u198"],"copy|Hazzard says short-term memory loss almost always resolves within a few weeks after the course (p1014).":["u253"],"copy|The average initial course in Hazzard is 3–6 treatments per week for 2–4 weeks.":["u254"],"copy|Short-term memory loss almost always resolves within a few weeks after the course ends (p1014).":["u255"],"copy|Hazzard gives 2–4 weeks for most SSRIs, SNRIs and mirtazapine and several months for tricyclics.":["u83"],"copy|It states that fluoxetine does not require tapering.":["u256"],"copy|For symptoms despite a long taper, the chapter suggests considering one dose of fluoxetine 20 mg (p1016).":["u257"]},"sequenceOffsets":[[258,-153],[346,-152],[350,-150],[401,0]]}
